Story 2B — External Identity Mapping Changes — hunt notes (Apr 15–Aug 15, 2026)

Ran the Story 2B baseline query against Oracle logon events for the last four months (607,782 events, 1,187 distinct db_users). Nothing in the results showed actual enterprise identity mapping in use — every account had empty EXTERNAL_USERID/GLOBAL_USERID and authenticated via local DATABASE auth, including SYS. So no federation abuse or IdP compromise indicators turned up, but SYS's numbers were odd enough to chase down.

SYS logged in 383,951 times over the window — about 63% of all logon activity in the index — from 201 different internal IPs. Most of those IPs (30+) look like normal long-running infrastructure, active the whole four months. Four of them didn't fit that pattern though: 10.68.123.113, 10.68.72.176, 10.65.68.72, and 10.65.68.84. Different subnet than the rest, lower logon counts, and one of them (10.68.123.113) only shows up once, in a three-hour window on 6/11, never again. That's the kind of shape you'd expect from a one-off credential misuse, so I pulled it apart.

Turned out to be RMAN. Once I pulled all the SQL these four hosts ran as SYS (62,636 statements total), most of it matched DBMS_STATS and LogMiner internal housekeeping right away. The rest — about 38,700 anonymous PL/SQL blocks — mostly turned out to call dbms_rcvman.* procedures once I deduped them down to their unique scripts, which is Oracle's own Recovery Manager package talking to the recovery catalog (archived logs, backup pieces, backup sets, etc.). The ALTER/CREATE statements from these hosts backed that up too — archive log switches, RMAN's dependency-mode session settings, and PFILE snapshots from SPFILE, all standard backup-job behavior. So I'm calling this one closed: legitimate scheduled RMAN backups running under SYS, not an attacker.

One thing from that same batch I'd still want a paper trail on: two ALTER PROFILE statements on 7/24 tightening password reuse settings on a profile called SERVICE_ACCOUNT (max reuse 24, reuse time 1 day). Looks like hardening, not something malicious, but it's a DB-wide security policy change made under SYS, so I'd feel better if there's a change ticket for it.

Still open going into next session: there's one PL/SQL script that only ran twice in the entire four months, and it looked like it was building dynamic SQL (variables named sql_stmt, prof_stmt, something referencing "ccdi"). The dedup query truncated the text before I could read the whole thing, so I never got to see what it actually does. That's the one loose end from this whole SYS thread — need to pull it in full:


