There was also a small script (15 hits) that looked like a compliance check on usernames/password age — low priority, didn't chase down who owns it.

Two things came up along the way that are worth their own tickets, separate from this hunt. First, a lot of the accounts I looked at early on — including some clearly shared/service accounts like CASHIER2 and BI_DESIGNER — are all authenticating with plain local database passwords instead of going through the enterprise identity provider. Might be totally expected for that account class, but it's worth someone confirming that and baselining what source IPs are normal for them. Second, and probably more important: several of the audit records I pulled had an actual event time over a year older than when they showed up in Splunk (events timestamped March/April 2025 landing in the index in mid-2026). Showed up on more than one host, so it doesn't look like a fluke. That's a real gap for detection — if audit data is sitting somewhere for over a year before it hits Splunk, we could be missing things happening right now. Flagging that for the pipeline team separately from this hunt.
